 - **The session can't change what Forge executes.** Its cwd is the main checkout and it has `Edit`/`Write`, so an agent misled by tracker or PR text could otherwise rewrite `commands.*` (which `forge run`/`forge verify` run through a shell) or `daemon.*` (picked up at the next spawn or resume). Three layers, innermost first:
   - *The CLI checks at the sink.* When a human starts the daemon (dashboard Start, or `forge dash` with `enabled: true`), the supervisor takes `trustDigest`: a sha256 of the checkout's realpath, `.forge/config.yml` and every file under `.forge/trackers/` (custom tracker modules `forge` imports). It hands the digest to the session as `FORGE_DAEMON_TRUST`. Under `FORGE_DAEMON=1`, every `forge` command recomputes it before reading config and refuses on a mismatch, or when the variable is missing. The check catches every route, including Bash (`git mv -f`, a redirect) and a fake checkout reached through `cd`. `forge init write`, `forge migrate` and `forge config set` refuse outright, because they write config.yml through `forge` itself. config.yml is read once, and the bytes that were hashed are the bytes parsed. A custom tracker module is checked again just before it is imported. Any symlink under `.forge/trackers/` is refused (its target could change without the link changing), and the walk never follows one. What the digest can't see: files a tracker module imports from outside `.forge/trackers/`, so keep tracker modules self-contained.
   - *The supervisor stops the session.* Every poll, and before any spawn, it compares the digest with the one taken at start. On a change it stops the child and stays stopped with an error in the dashboard; respawns after an idle stop or a failed resume reuse the old snapshot, so a change made during a turn is never blessed by a restart. A spawn hashes config.yml once and builds argv from those same bytes, so a file swapped and restored between two reads can't slip in. Only a human's Start takes a new snapshot, and only when no child is running. A human editing config while the daemon runs gets the same stop, which is the price of not being able to tell the two apart.
